# Purpose

This document defines how the hubIO repository is organized, configured, developed, and maintained.

It establishes the shared development context for every engineer and AI assistant without redefining engineering principles already established by the Engineering Constitution.

---

# Scope

This standard defines:

* Repository organization
* Technology stack
* Development environment
* Repository conventions
* Development workflow
* Release workflow

It intentionally excludes product strategy and implementation-specific engineering guidance.

---

# Constitutional Interpretation

This document extends the hubIO Engineering Constitution.

It provides repository-specific context while relying on the Constitution for engineering philosophy, decision making, and quality expectations.

---

# Required Reading

1. Engineering Constitution

Recommended:

* Product Standards
* Architecture Standards

---

# Engineering Responsibilities

This document owns:

* Repository structure
* Development environment
* Technology stack
* Repository conventions
* Development workflow
* Release workflow

It does not define product strategy or implementation standards.

---

# Repository Overview

The hubIO repository contains the complete source code, configuration, documentation, and engineering assets required to develop, test, and deploy the platform.

The repository is designed to support long-term scalability, maintainability, and collaborative development while serving as the single source of truth for the entire product.

The repository should remain:

* Well-structured
* Consistent
* Predictable
* Easy to navigate
* Easy to maintain
* AI-friendly

Every file, directory, and configuration should have a clear responsibility.

Avoid unnecessary complexity, duplicated logic, and ambiguous project organization.

Repository organization should prioritize long-term maintainability over short-term convenience.

---

# Repository Structure

The repository should be organized into clearly defined domains with strict separation of responsibilities.

Each directory must own a single engineering concern and avoid overlapping responsibilities with other modules.

The repository structure should encourage modular development and make features easy to locate, understand, and extend.

A typical repository includes:

* Application source code
* Shared components
* Shared libraries
* Database resources
* Static assets
* Documentation
* Engineering Standards
* Configuration files
* Build and deployment configuration
* Development tooling

Engineering Standards under `.cursor/rules` are considered part of the repository and should evolve together with the source code.

Repository organization should remain stable as the product grows, minimizing disruptive structural changes.

---

# Technology Stack

Technology choices should remain intentional, stable, and aligned with the long-term goals of the product.

New technologies should only be introduced when they provide clear architectural, operational, or developer experience benefits.

The project currently uses the following technology stack.

## Frontend

* Next.js (App Router)
* TypeScript
* React
* Tailwind CSS
* shadcn/ui
* Motion
* React Hook Form
* Zod
* TanStack Table
* Lucide Icons

## Backend

* Supabase
* PostgreSQL

## Hosting

* Vercel

## Development Tooling

* Cursor
* Git
* GitHub
* ESLint
* Prettier

Technology selection should prioritize:

* Stability
* Maintainability
* Developer Experience
* Performance
* Scalability
* Strong community support

Avoid introducing overlapping frameworks, unnecessary dependencies, or technologies that duplicate existing capabilities.

---

# Development Environment

Every engineer should be able to reproduce the same development environment with minimal setup.

Development environments should remain consistent across all contributors to reduce environment-specific issues.

The local environment should provide:

* Fast startup
* Hot reload
* Reliable debugging
* Consistent formatting
* Static analysis
* Type checking

Development should always be performed against local environment variables rather than production configuration.

Configuration should remain deterministic and reproducible.

---

# Environment Variables

Environment configuration must remain external to the source code.

Sensitive values must never be committed into the repository.

Environment variables should be:

* Clearly documented
* Named consistently
* Grouped by responsibility
* Validated during application startup when appropriate

Recommended categories include:

* Application
* Database
* Authentication
* Storage
* Third-party Integrations
* Analytics
* Feature Flags

Example environment files:

* `.env.local`
* `.env.example`

The `.env.example` file should document every required environment variable without exposing sensitive values.

---

# Package Management

The project should maintain a consistent dependency management strategy across the entire repository.

Only one package manager should be used throughout the project to ensure deterministic installations and reproducible builds.

Package dependencies should remain:

* Necessary
* Well-maintained
* Actively supported
* Compatible with the current technology stack

Avoid adding dependencies that duplicate existing functionality or introduce unnecessary complexity.

Dependencies should be reviewed regularly and updated in a controlled manner.

Development dependencies should remain separate from production dependencies whenever possible.

---

# Development Scripts

Repository scripts should provide a consistent interface for common development tasks.

Every script should have a single, well-defined responsibility and produce predictable results.

Typical development scripts include:

* Development server
* Production build
* Preview build
* Linting
* Formatting
* Type checking
* Testing
* Database generation or migration
* Seed data
* Cleanup tasks

Scripts should be documented and named consistently across the project.

Automation should always be preferred over manual execution whenever possible.

---

# Branch Strategy

The repository follows a trunk-based development workflow with short-lived feature branches.

Branches should remain focused on a single change or feature.

Recommended branch naming:

* `feature/<name>`
* `fix/<name>`
* `refactor/<name>`
* `docs/<name>`
* `chore/<name>`
* `release/<version>`

Avoid long-lived branches that diverge significantly from the main branch.

The default branch should always remain stable and deployable.

---

# Commit Convention

Commit history should communicate intent clearly and remain easy to understand.

Each commit should represent a single logical change.

Recommended commit types:

* feat
* fix
* refactor
* docs
* style
* test
* chore
* ci
* perf
* revert

Commit messages should:

* Be concise
* Describe the purpose of the change
* Use the imperative mood
* Avoid unrelated modifications within a single commit

A clean commit history improves code reviews, debugging, and release management.

---

# Pull Request Workflow

Every Pull Request should represent a complete, reviewable unit of work.

Pull Requests exist to improve software quality through collaboration, knowledge sharing, and engineering review.

Each Pull Request should:

* Address a single objective
* Be easy to review
* Include meaningful descriptions
* Reference related issues when applicable
* Pass all automated quality checks

Before requesting review, every Pull Request should:

* Build successfully
* Pass linting
* Pass type checking
* Pass all tests
* Follow the Engineering Constitution
* Comply with all applicable Engineering Standards

Large Pull Requests should be divided into smaller, independently reviewable changes whenever possible.

---

# Release Strategy

Releases should be predictable, repeatable, and low risk.

Every release should represent a stable state of the repository.

The release process should prioritize:

* Stability
* Backward compatibility
* Deployment confidence
* Minimal operational disruption

Breaking changes should be intentional, documented, and communicated before release.

Feature development should be completed and validated before becoming part of a production release.

Every production deployment should be traceable to a specific version of the repository.

---

# Deployment Overview

The project uses automated deployment pipelines to ensure consistent releases.

Deployment should be:

* Automated
* Repeatable
* Observable
* Recoverable

Environment-specific configuration should remain outside the application source code.

Production deployments should occur only after all quality gates have been satisfied.

Deployment processes should support rapid rollback when unexpected issues occur.

Operational monitoring should continue after deployment to detect regressions and system failures.

---

# Configuration Principles

Repository configuration should remain centralized, predictable, and easy to maintain.

Configuration files should:

* Have a single responsibility
* Follow consistent naming conventions
* Avoid duplicated settings
* Be version controlled whenever appropriate

Application behavior should be controlled through configuration rather than source code modifications whenever practical.

Sensitive configuration must remain external to the repository.

Configuration should remain understandable for both engineers and AI assistants.

---

# Development Lifecycle

Development follows an iterative engineering workflow.

Typical lifecycle:

1. Understand requirements.
2. Review applicable Engineering Standards.
3. Design the solution.
4. Implement the feature.
5. Perform self-review.
6. Execute automated quality checks.
7. Submit for review.
8. Merge after approval.
9. Deploy through the release pipeline.
10. Monitor production behavior.

Every engineering activity should begin with understanding the relevant standards before implementation.

---

# Project Quality Gates

The repository remains healthy when:

* Repository structure remains organized.
* Technology choices remain consistent.
* Development workflows remain standardized.
* Configuration remains maintainable.
* Build and deployment processes remain reliable.
* Documentation accurately reflects the current repository.
* Engineering Standards remain synchronized with repository evolution.

This document defines repository and development standards.

Product strategy is defined by the Product Standards.

Implementation standards are defined by the Engineering Constitution and the relevant Engineering Standards.
